        {
          "id": "scm_call120_cirrus_ice_only_layers_20_59",
          "description": "Reference: WRF 4.8 em_scm_xy single-column run (GNU real32 build, dt = 60 s, 59 layers, mp = 6), call 120 (20:00 UTC) dumped by the instrumented ctessum-claude/WRF fork (module_esm_dump with esm_dump_inner hooks inside mp_wsm6_run of the ctessum-claude/MMM-physics fork, commit 52cfbd7) and replayed through the real64 (kind_phys = real64) standalone kernels/wsm6_driver. INPUTS are the kernel's input state (t, qi, qr, qs, qg, den, delz of tile column 1), i.e. the state the fallout block sees at the start of the sub-step. REFERENCE FLUXES are Fortran-derived: the kernel's fall arrays (fall(:,k,:) = post-fallout density x fall speed / delz for k >= 2, and the semi-Lagrangian surface precipitation / delz / dtcld at k = 1), multiplied by delz, from three real64 replays at ESM_DT = 0.04, 0.02, 0.01 s and Richardson-extrapolated to dt -> 0 (second order; the kernel's fluxes converge linearly in dt to the donor-cell fluxes rho v_t q, so the extrapolation removes the residual to ~1e-12 relative). REFERENCE TENDENCIES are WRF's own fallout operator in the dt -> 0 limit: nislfv_rain_plm, extracted verbatim from mp_wsm6.F90 (lines 1879-2122) into the real64 harness data/eqwefic/plm_check, run on this test's input column -- the falling density den*q with the round-off negatives WRF keeps, and fall speeds v = F/(rho max(q,0)) from the reference fluxes above, zeroed where WRF zeroes them (workr where q_r <= 0, worka where q_s + q_g <= 1e-15, work1c where q_i <= 0) -- at dt = 2e-3, 1e-3 and 5e-4 s, Richardson-extrapolated to dt -> 0 (it agrees with the extrapolation from 4e-3 .. 1e-3 s to <= 6e-8 of each column's peak) and divided by rho. Each category is replayed on its own, so melting and the rest of the sub-step cannot contaminate it, which the kernel's whole-sub-step rain increment could not guarantee. nislfv_rain_plm6, which sediments snow and graupel, is nislfv_rain_plm applied to each at the shared speed (checked by diff: only its mean-speed iteration differs, and that vanishes as dt -> 0). The exception is the top two layers of the column: WRF empties them on every call by a dt-independent amount (FORTRAN_BUGS B16), so they have no dt -> 0 limit, and their reference is the rule's conservative value (every category carries < 1e-12 kg m^-3 there in this column). This replaced, on 2026-09-22, the donor-cell FORMULA the tendencies used to pin, which is 59 % (ice) away from WRF's operator (Linf over the column maximum) in this column. Cloud-ice fluxes have no kernel counterpart above the surface (fallc is dumped only at k = 1), so F_i is checked against the same formulae evaluated by the filler on the input state (N_i = min(max(5.38e7 (rho q_i)^0.75, 1e3), 1e6), D = min(dicon sqrt(rho q_i/N_i), dimax), v_i = 1.49e4 D^1.31) -- a wiring check, not an independent reference. The ice TENDENCY is WRF's operator applied to those formula speeds, and on this column it is also checked against the whole kernel: it agrees with the Richardson extrapolation to dtcld -> 0 of (qi_rates - qi_in)/dtcld from the dtcld = 0.01, 0.02 and 0.04 s real64 wsm6_driver replays of the same column (data/eqwefic/dumps/wsm6/scm_ref_120_driver_dt*.json; nothing melts or freezes here, so that is the fallout alone; TENDENCY_ERROR_BUDGET.md) to 4.5e-7 of the peak below the top two layers. Real64 replay fluxes agree with the real32 in-model fall arrays to ~2e-6 relative (the dumped pvtr/pvts/pvtg are real32). Residual esm-vs-extrapolated mismatch (real32 constants and default-real literals of the kernel, FORTRAN_BUGS.md N2; measured with zero tolerances): fluxes and tendencies <= 1.1e-7 of each column's maximum (rain 8.8e-8, snow 1.1e-7, graupel 1.1e-7, ice 1.0e-7), surface rates 1.1-1.3e-7 relative -- the same floor as the rate models (the kernel's real32 pvtr/pvts/pvtg, bvtr = 0.800000011920929, den0, 1.49e4, 1.31 and 5.38e7 versus the exact esm constants); column tolerances are 1e-6 of each reference column's maximum (Linf_error), scalar tolerances rel 2e-6, i.e. 5-10x above. Measured again with zero tolerances after the switch to the jh2010 rule: the rain, snow and graupel tendencies agree with WRF's operator to <= 1.14e-7 of the column maximum, the same real32 floor, and their tolerance is 1e-6 of the maximum. The ICE tendency agrees to <= 2.7e-7 of its maximum and carries the same 1e-6 tolerance. That needed the upwind choice of donor side added to EarthSciDiscretizations PR #42 on 2026-09-22 (commit 14b5db5): where the third-order interface speed overshoots below zero at the edge of an ice layer (3-12 faces per column in these tests), WRF's arrival-point remap takes the mass crossing that face from the TOP face of the layer below; before it the ice residual reached 6.4e-6 and the tolerance was 3e-5. What remains is not the operator (driven with the kernel's own fall speeds, it matches WRF's fallout to 1e-8 - 4e-8 of the ice maximum) but this component's ice fall flux F_i, which differs from the kernel's by 4e-8 - 6e-8 of its peak. Regime: SCM cirrus column, WRF layers 20-59 (the component's 40-layer window; the window's lowest face is the top of layer 19, where the kernel's interior flux fall(:,20,:) delz applies as the donor flux): only cloud ice (<= 4.6e-5 kg/kg in layers 50-57) is present, so the rain, snow and graupel fluxes, tendencies and precipitation are exactly zero and the ice tendency is checked by the formula.",
